982
00:50:57.420 --> 00:51:00.000
And I remember he had to leave set.

983
00:51:00.520 --> 00:51:06.180
And I don't know if he went to the hospital or to an eye doctor, but he had a scratched cornea.

984
00:51:06.180 --> 00:51:11.460
Yeah, I thought they took him to a local, like the clinic, the nearest hospital.

985
00:51:11.700 --> 00:51:21.660
Every single day when you're filming, whether it's on set or location, they put in the call sheet the location where you're filming and the nearest hospital's location.

986
00:51:22.300 --> 00:51:22.660
Yeah.

987
00:51:22.860 --> 00:51:25.760
They researched that ahead of time for this reason.

988
00:51:25.900 --> 00:51:29.820
So I think they took him to the nearest, whatever that was, clinic, right?

989
00:51:30.240 --> 00:51:30.680
Yeah.

990
00:51:30.800 --> 00:51:34.240
And he was in quite a bit of pain and he was like, no, it's still in there.

991
00:51:34.240 --> 00:51:35.660
There's something wrong.

992
00:51:35.880 --> 00:51:38.900
You know, that moment in your body where you're like, it's not right.

993
00:51:39.060 --> 00:51:39.880
Something's not right.

994
00:51:40.140 --> 00:51:45.000
And it had, in fact, scratched his cornea and they treated him.

995
00:51:45.120 --> 00:51:48.080
And then he came back and finished filming because he's such a trooper.

996
00:51:48.980 --> 00:51:51.300
Well, I've had this happen to me, Angela.

997
00:51:51.640 --> 00:51:56.800
I got a piece of sand in my eye by using a face scrub.

998
00:51:57.000 --> 00:51:57.600
Oh, Lord.

999
00:51:57.920 --> 00:51:58.660
With sand in it.

1000
00:52:00.080 --> 00:52:07.460
And I got a piece in my eye and I was like, it feels like something's in your eye when you scratch your cornea.

1001
00:52:07.660 --> 00:52:09.380
But I couldn't find it.

1002
00:52:09.560 --> 00:52:12.920
So I went to an eye doctor and I had scratched my cornea.

1003
00:52:13.240 --> 00:52:15.640
So I feel for Leslie.

1004
00:52:15.640 --> 00:52:18.180
It is a very, it is a not fun feeling.

1005
00:52:18.180 --> 00:52:25.680
I have never scratched my cornea, but one time I was eating jalapeno potato chips because I love them so much.

1006
00:52:25.840 --> 00:52:36.520
And I bit into it kind of aggressively and the jalapeno dust off the chip went into my eyes and it was very painful.

1007
00:52:36.940 --> 00:52:37.900
Why are you laughing?

1008
00:52:38.240 --> 00:52:39.400
This really happened.

1009
00:52:39.400 --> 00:52:50.180
And I bit into the chip and then I was like, why am I laughing for that story and not laughing?

1010
00:52:50.340 --> 00:52:50.720
I don't know.

1011
00:52:50.960 --> 00:52:51.520
I don't know.

1012
00:52:51.900 --> 00:52:52.220
Okay.

1013
00:52:52.300 --> 00:52:53.560
We should probably get back on topic.

1014
00:52:54.700 --> 00:52:55.060
Yeah.

1015
00:52:55.300 --> 00:52:56.440
Well, here's the thing.

1016
00:52:56.480 --> 00:53:03.560
I remember Leslie leaving set, but I don't remember when exactly it happened or how it happened.

1017
00:53:03.800 --> 00:53:05.080
I just know he got sand in his eye.

1018
00:53:05.440 --> 00:53:05.700
All right.

1019
00:53:05.800 --> 00:53:06.800
This is what I think happened.

1020
00:53:06.800 --> 00:53:16.880
And I watched the deleted scenes and there's a moment, it's not in the episode, where Dwight is wrestling with Stanley and knocks him to the ground and is like, take that, old man.

1021
00:53:17.040 --> 00:53:19.660
And he kind of kicks at the sand, right?

1022
00:53:20.360 --> 00:53:21.140
Oh, yes.

1023
00:53:21.140 --> 00:53:22.180
I'm remembering this.

1024
00:53:22.260 --> 00:53:28.640
And I think then Rain felt really bad because he realized in that moment, that's when we thought it happened.

1025
00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:30.560
Yes.

1026
00:53:30.940 --> 00:53:31.860
That's right.

1027
00:53:32.300 --> 00:53:32.660
Yeah.

1028
00:53:32.820 --> 00:53:33.460
That's right.

1029
00:53:34.040 --> 00:53:36.240
Well, this was not the only injury.

1030
00:53:36.800 --> 00:53:42.740
From this episode, Ed hurt his finger during the sumo wrestling.

1031
00:53:42.900 --> 00:53:43.320
Do you remember that?

1032
00:53:43.380 --> 00:53:45.520
I think he like lost a fingernail or something.

1033
00:53:46.140 --> 00:53:49.400
He was like, sorry, it makes me laugh so hard.

1034
00:53:49.620 --> 00:53:50.640
I shouldn't laugh.

1035
00:53:50.760 --> 00:53:56.400
But it's when that moment, if you guys watch it, when Andy and Dwight really start going at each other.

1036
00:53:56.540 --> 00:53:59.960
Like Dwight walks over and he's eating a sandwich, even though he's eating all these hot dogs.

1037
00:53:59.960 --> 00:54:03.040
He walks over and then they just start going at each other.

1038
00:54:03.160 --> 00:54:05.240
And Ed is like kind of flailing his arms at him.

1039
00:54:05.840 --> 00:54:11.220
Somehow in this tussle, Ed broke his fingernail, but he said he broke it really bad.

1040
00:54:11.340 --> 00:54:11.980
Like it ripped.

1041
00:54:12.160 --> 00:54:14.320
I think it like ripped below the...

1042
00:54:14.320 --> 00:54:15.580
That's what I remember.

1043
00:54:15.600 --> 00:54:15.980
Yeah.

1044
00:54:15.980 --> 00:54:17.440
Like it was gruesome looking.

1045
00:54:17.440 --> 00:54:17.800
It was gruesome.

1046
00:54:17.940 --> 00:54:20.680
But Ed was like yelling in his sumo suit.

1047
00:54:20.800 --> 00:54:22.020
He was yelling, stop, stop.

1048
00:54:22.300 --> 00:54:25.220
And all he could get out was, I broke my nail.

1049
00:54:25.220 --> 00:54:28.500
And people were like, okay, did you break your fingernail?

1050
00:54:28.760 --> 00:54:31.440
And he's like, no, guys, I broke my nail.

1051
00:54:32.100 --> 00:54:32.920
Oh, no.

1052
00:54:33.880 --> 00:54:34.700
Oh, no.

1053
00:54:35.940 --> 00:54:37.220
But he didn't leave set.

1054
00:54:37.440 --> 00:54:38.380
No, he didn't leave set.

1055
00:54:38.520 --> 00:54:39.640
They came over and they...

1056
00:54:39.640 --> 00:54:42.140
But there was that moment when someone was like, what happened?

1057
00:54:42.260 --> 00:54:43.280
Like Ed broke his nail.

1058
00:54:43.360 --> 00:54:46.280
And he's like, no, it's more than just a broken nail.

1059
00:54:46.460 --> 00:54:48.140
And then they wrapped it up.

1060
00:54:48.920 --> 00:54:53.480
Well, now, Angela, we're going to get into a big moment.

1061
00:54:53.820 --> 00:54:55.460
This was huge for our show.

1062
00:54:55.600 --> 00:54:56.720
This is a huge stunt.

1063
00:54:56.720 --> 00:55:01.660
You have to remember, our show never left a little tiny office.

1064
00:55:01.900 --> 00:55:03.920
So we've got to break down.

1065
00:55:04.060 --> 00:55:05.360
We've got two big things coming up.

1066
00:55:05.480 --> 00:55:09.940
We've got this Andy floating away and then the coals.

1067
00:55:10.220 --> 00:55:11.400
So, Jenna, let's start with Andy.

1068
00:55:12.180 --> 00:55:12.420
Okay.

1069
00:55:12.860 --> 00:55:15.140
Andy walks over to the water.

1070
00:55:15.320 --> 00:55:20.060
And we had a fan question about this from Allie Jo Higginbotham, who said,

1071
00:55:20.700 --> 00:55:23.680
when Andy walks to the lake, he has something yellow in his hand.

1072
00:55:23.860 --> 00:55:24.580
What is he holding?

1073
00:55:24.580 --> 00:55:27.260
And what is he planning to do with it at the shore?

1074
00:55:27.840 --> 00:55:30.840
Well, he's holding a...

1075
00:55:30.840 --> 00:55:31.800
A bandana.

1076
00:55:32.100 --> 00:55:33.400
He's holding a yellow bandana.

1077
00:55:33.540 --> 00:55:36.060
Michael had handed these out in the egg race.

1078
00:55:36.260 --> 00:55:39.260
And that's what everyone had used to cover their eyes.

1079
00:55:39.440 --> 00:55:42.260
And Andy is holding his yellow bandana from the egg race.

1080
00:55:42.400 --> 00:55:43.280
And he's going to...

1081
00:55:43.280 --> 00:55:47.700
He hopes to dip it in the water to then maybe wrap it around his neck to cool himself off.

1082
00:55:47.820 --> 00:55:48.700
This was the idea.

1083
00:55:49.480 --> 00:55:49.900
Yes.

1084
00:55:50.080 --> 00:55:53.640
But instead, he falls in the water and starts floating away.

1085
00:55:54.260 --> 00:55:58.900
When he fell in the water, you know, I wasn't too far off because my character witnesses this.

1086
00:55:59.180 --> 00:55:59.700
Yes.

1087
00:56:00.040 --> 00:56:04.800
And there is a take that didn't make it that he literally went...

1088
00:56:04.800 --> 00:56:06.180
He like rolled, Jenna.

1089
00:56:06.500 --> 00:56:09.000
He like rolled into the water.

1090
00:56:09.380 --> 00:56:15.020
But they used this take because it was more clear why he was going down there.

1091
00:56:15.180 --> 00:56:19.000
He's trying to hang that bandana in the water, trying to balance.

1092
00:56:19.820 --> 00:56:30.520
Well, let me say, and we got another frequently asked question from Candice Kelso, Michael Lung, Olive Taylor, and Phoebe Bono.

1093
00:56:30.920 --> 00:56:34.560
Was that really Ed in the water or was that a stunt double?

1094
00:56:34.840 --> 00:56:36.780
Guys, that was Ed in the water.

1095
00:56:37.040 --> 00:56:38.280
Ed did it all.

1096
00:56:38.480 --> 00:56:40.360
I don't know that they offered him a stunt double.

1097
00:56:40.680 --> 00:56:41.300
I don't know.

1098
00:56:41.300 --> 00:56:48.240
So, Kent told me they offered him a stunt double, but Ed insisted on doing all of the floating in the water himself.

1099
00:56:48.700 --> 00:56:53.620
He was out there floating for over 45 minutes.

1100
00:56:54.240 --> 00:57:04.320
And I'm thinking maybe when he was like, oh, no, I'll fall in the water, like maybe he didn't realize what that all entailed.

1101
00:57:04.640 --> 00:57:05.120
Yeah.

1102
00:57:05.340 --> 00:57:11.680
And perhaps having a stunt double on the side to complete some of the very far away floating.

1103
00:57:12.100 --> 00:57:16.960
But guys, that is Ed the whole time floating out there.

1104
00:57:17.140 --> 00:57:20.780
Not only in the day, but then he had to do it again at night.

1105
00:57:21.620 --> 00:57:27.160
This was two different shoots for him in the water, a day scene and a night scene.

1106
00:57:27.960 --> 00:57:35.700
Now, when he was out there at night, he did have a thin wetsuit on underneath all that sumo gear to keep him warm.

1107
00:57:35.720 --> 00:57:37.060
So they did think of that.

1108
00:57:37.200 --> 00:57:47.180
But before we get too far into this stunt, Angela, we also had fan questions from Rebecca Gonzalez, Connor N., and Maura Anderson, who said,

1109
00:57:47.180 --> 00:57:51.740
Angela, how did you make it through the scene of Andy floating away?

1110
00:57:52.580 --> 00:57:54.140
It was so hard.

1111
00:57:54.340 --> 00:57:55.660
It was that's what she said.

1112
00:57:56.440 --> 00:57:57.280
Oh, boy.

1113
00:57:57.380 --> 00:57:57.860
I'm sorry.

1114
00:57:58.180 --> 00:58:09.940
It was really it was really difficult, difficult not to laugh, really difficult, especially because Ed was improvising a little bit as he floated away.

1115
00:58:09.940 --> 00:58:13.040
Some of that dialogue was not scripted.

1116
00:58:13.200 --> 00:58:19.040
So, you know, when I'm like, I don't really understand what it is you're saying to me that was scripted.

1117
00:58:19.220 --> 00:58:25.560
But then Ed, Ed was physically floating away and he was dealing with the elements and he improvised this line.

1118
00:58:25.840 --> 00:58:30.480
Look at what I'm doing and go tell somebody it.

1119
00:58:30.940 --> 00:58:33.160
I love that line so much.

1120
00:58:33.400 --> 00:58:35.020
That was Ed improvising.

1121
00:58:35.640 --> 00:58:37.500
That is awesome.

1122
00:58:37.500 --> 00:58:38.100
I know.

1123
00:58:38.320 --> 00:58:39.260
So it was really hard.

1124
00:58:39.360 --> 00:58:43.120
And I didn't want to laugh because poor Ed is floating out in that water.

1125
00:58:43.280 --> 00:58:44.040
You know what I mean?

1126
00:58:44.460 --> 00:58:44.840
Yes.

1127
00:58:44.840 --> 00:58:47.640
If you laugh, you ruin that moment.

1128
00:58:47.640 --> 00:58:48.040
Yes.

1129
00:58:48.140 --> 00:58:49.560
And he has to float longer.

1130
00:58:49.720 --> 00:58:50.560
I was working hard.

1131
00:58:50.560 --> 00:58:52.340
I was working hard not to laugh.

1132
00:58:53.260 --> 00:58:58.480
Well, we asked Jen about Ed in the water and here's what she had to say.

1133
00:58:58.860 --> 00:59:02.020
Oh, shooting the Andy floating away scenes was really fun.

1134
00:59:02.220 --> 00:59:03.900
I love that part of the script.

1135
00:59:03.900 --> 00:59:10.160
I love when Angela pretends that she doesn't understand what he's saying.

1136
00:59:10.160 --> 00:59:11.480
That was so much fun to write.

1137
00:59:13.020 --> 00:59:22.280
I remember that Ed was like in the sumo suit and then there was a rope attached to him and he was being pulled by a boat.

1138
00:59:22.440 --> 00:59:26.460
And then at a certain point, the boat would leave him and then he would just drift.

1139
00:59:26.460 --> 00:59:32.820
But there was also a current, I believe the water was also cold and he had to keep his head up.

1140
00:59:33.000 --> 00:59:39.820
And I remember that it was a challenging thing because his head would sort of get lost and it would just like go under unless he helped,

1141
00:59:39.820 --> 00:59:43.380
he put his neck up and just try to hold it up.

1142
00:59:43.380 --> 00:59:44.860
So I think it was a difficult thing.

1143
00:59:45.060 --> 00:59:51.940
Also, the timing of the boat and the timing of us trying to get it on camera with Michael's talking head was difficult.

1144
00:59:52.300 --> 01:00:00.600
So I remember that Ed was like a real trooper and there were a lot of discussions about like how far out he should be at sea because we wanted to see him.

1145
01:00:00.860 --> 01:00:07.200
We didn't want to be too obvious that it was him and Michael might see him, but we didn't want him to be so far away that he was this tiny little dot.

1146
01:00:07.200 --> 01:00:11.140
So Ed was in the water for quite a while while we tried to work this out.

1147
01:00:12.240 --> 01:00:18.380
And the other thing I remember, which is crazy to say now, but there was a safety meeting right before it.

1148
01:00:18.500 --> 01:00:28.880
And I feel like the very beginning of the safety meeting, like Ed was there, but like he was just getting there because everything was so rushed.

1149
01:00:28.880 --> 01:00:37.180
And I think the safety person said one thing, like as Ed was approaching that I wasn't sure Ed clocked and I had this moment of, should I tell him or not?

1150
01:00:37.640 --> 01:00:43.140
And I remember the safety guy talking about water snakes and I was like, oh my God, oh my God.

1151
01:00:43.240 --> 01:00:44.360
Do I repeat this to Ed?

1152
01:00:44.480 --> 01:00:45.380
Do I not repeat this to Ed?

1153
01:00:45.460 --> 01:00:49.920
I don't think I repeated it to Ed because he was going to be in this water.

1154
01:00:50.160 --> 01:00:51.800
It was going to be very cold.

1155
01:00:52.300 --> 01:00:59.200
Um, I thought, would I want to know about water snakes before I got into the water in a sumo suit or not?

1156
01:00:59.340 --> 01:01:02.160
So, um, yeah, I think I made that decision.

1157
01:01:02.580 --> 01:01:05.060
Tell me if you think that was the correct decision to make.

1158
01:01:05.440 --> 01:01:08.260
Anyway, so there were a lot of challenges of shooting that.

1159
01:01:08.460 --> 01:01:12.980
There were no, uh, snake events during the shooting of that sequence.

1160
01:01:12.980 --> 01:01:24.680
Uh, and I thought Ed did a phenomenal job and so did Angela and I'm just in love with that sequence of, um, events, but it was, uh, challenging to shoot.

1161
01:01:25.660 --> 01:01:26.260
Wow.

1162
01:01:26.820 --> 01:01:27.280
Ed.

1163
01:01:27.840 --> 01:01:28.340
Ed.

1164
01:01:29.600 --> 01:01:31.160
Possibility of water snakes.

1165
01:01:31.340 --> 01:01:32.180
Oh my God.

1166
01:01:32.900 --> 01:01:33.380
Oh boy.

1167
01:01:34.540 --> 01:01:34.920
Oh boy.

1168
01:01:35.460 --> 01:01:35.720
Jen Salata.

1169
01:01:37.120 --> 01:01:43.700
Well, now Jen mentioned in her clip that they would have to pull Ed behind a boat to get him in place.

1170
01:01:44.380 --> 01:01:48.320
So Kent told me that power boats were not allowed on the lake.

1171
01:01:48.640 --> 01:01:52.840
So they had to have a stunt coordinator in a row boat.

1172
01:01:53.000 --> 01:01:54.260
I remember, Jenna.

1173
01:01:54.620 --> 01:01:55.860
I remember rowing him.

1174
01:01:56.120 --> 01:01:56.240
Yes.

1175
01:01:56.540 --> 01:02:05.060
Can you imagine if you're somewhere else at this park and you're just hiking or maybe you're riding a horse from the equestrian center.

1176
01:02:05.060 --> 01:02:13.020
And you look out onto the lake and you see a man in a row boat rowing another man by a rope who's in a sumo suit.

1177
01:02:13.120 --> 01:02:14.760
What are you thinking when you see that?

1178
01:02:14.880 --> 01:02:15.360
I don't know.

1179
01:02:15.400 --> 01:02:16.340
What goes to your head?

1180
01:02:16.340 --> 01:02:16.740
I don't know.

1181
01:02:16.880 --> 01:02:17.560
I don't know.

1182
01:02:17.860 --> 01:02:18.700
Are you worried?

1183
01:02:18.700 --> 01:02:19.540
What are you thinking?

1184
01:02:19.920 --> 01:02:26.340
So I watched this play out because we would have to reset Ed to that spot where we would have dialogue.

1185
01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:29.300
And inevitably he'd float away.

1186
01:02:29.640 --> 01:02:29.760
Right?

1187
01:02:29.760 --> 01:02:32.820
And then they would, the guy, there he was.

1188
01:02:33.000 --> 01:02:35.740
And I had to make sure I didn't look at the row boat guy.

1189
01:02:35.940 --> 01:02:36.080
Right?

1190
01:02:36.340 --> 01:02:40.640
You know, like I couldn't have my eyes dart away.

1191
01:02:40.880 --> 01:02:45.200
But off in the periphery of my vision was this guy in a boat.

1192
01:02:45.440 --> 01:02:48.180
And he would row and pull Ed.

1193
01:02:48.380 --> 01:02:54.320
Ed had a harness suit on under the sumo suit.

1194
01:02:54.320 --> 01:02:59.840
And they put through the arm of the sumo suit a fishing line.

1195
01:03:00.760 --> 01:03:03.120
And this guy would pull Ed.

1196
01:03:03.200 --> 01:03:07.780
And as he did, Jenna, I would see water break around his body.

1197
01:03:08.000 --> 01:03:10.960
You know, like if you hold something in the water.

1198
01:03:11.420 --> 01:03:11.760
Yeah.

1199
01:03:11.840 --> 01:03:18.720
And Ed said that a majority of that water as he was being pulled would flush through the suit.

1200
01:03:18.960 --> 01:03:19.960
Oh, my God.

1201
01:03:19.960 --> 01:03:21.180
And out his neck.

1202
01:03:21.440 --> 01:03:33.300
Well, Kent told me if you look carefully in the Michael talking head where Ed is floating in the background, you will see that there's a pretty strong current in the water.

1203
01:03:33.300 --> 01:03:35.440
But Ed is not moving.

1204
01:03:35.440 --> 01:03:44.040
That is because at that point, the only way to keep him in the shot was to just keep him attached to the boat.

1205
01:03:44.240 --> 01:03:53.380
And so he said, if you look for it, he's very oddly staying in one place while a pretty heavy current is going underneath him.

1206
01:03:53.800 --> 01:03:55.320
So, so insane.

1207
01:03:56.760 --> 01:04:03.700
Angela, I also got the story about these sumo suits from Carrie Bennett, our wardrobe designer.

1208
01:04:03.940 --> 01:04:05.160
Oh, let's hear it.

1209
01:04:05.440 --> 01:04:13.200
So she said that this was one of the most difficult costume challenges she had for our show.

1210
01:04:13.380 --> 01:04:16.820
She had to create these inflatable sumo suits.

1211
01:04:17.340 --> 01:04:26.360
But because she knew Andy would end up floating away in it, whatever she put them in had to be safe for him to float on water.

1212
01:04:27.100 --> 01:04:34.100
So she said, as soon as she read the script, she immediately went to Greg and said, I cannot design a costume that will allow someone to float on water.

1213
01:04:34.260 --> 01:04:36.140
I need a stunt person to help me.

1214
01:04:37.280 --> 01:04:47.500
So she got together with the stunt person and they realized that kind of like what Jen said, the biggest challenge was keeping his head from going underwater.

1215
01:04:47.500 --> 01:04:53.140
Because she was like, his body would float, but the head would go under.

1216
01:04:53.620 --> 01:04:59.660
So that is why the guys are wearing those crazy head pieces in the episode.

1217
01:04:59.660 --> 01:05:01.640
Those were custom made, right?

1218
01:05:02.360 --> 01:05:02.800
Yes.

1219
01:05:03.020 --> 01:05:03.160
Yeah.

1220
01:05:03.160 --> 01:05:15.020
She said, what if I made a floaty helmet in the shape of like a sumo wrestler's bun and the bun is actually a floating device.

1221
01:05:15.620 --> 01:05:16.220
Brilliant.

1222
01:05:17.040 --> 01:05:23.100
So she designed this extra bit of safety for Ed with this floaty bun.

1223
01:05:23.280 --> 01:05:31.460
And before we shot, they went over to Kent Sabornak's pool and they put a stunt guy in the suit and they tested it out.

1224
01:05:31.820 --> 01:05:33.020
And it worked.

1225
01:05:33.280 --> 01:05:34.260
Oh, my gosh.

1226
01:05:34.420 --> 01:05:39.780
You're talking about what happens when like someone's riding a horse by and Ed's floating and there's a guy with a rowboat.

1227
01:05:39.960 --> 01:05:41.320
What about Kent's family?

1228
01:05:42.160 --> 01:05:44.460
Hey, Dad, who's in the pool?

1229
01:05:45.540 --> 01:05:46.720
Don't worry about it.

1230
01:05:46.880 --> 01:05:48.300
It's a little thing for work.

1231
01:05:50.220 --> 01:05:50.780
Totally.

1232
01:05:51.660 --> 01:05:52.220
Totally.

1233
01:05:53.280 --> 01:05:57.500
Also, when these guys in the writer's room wrote this sequence.

1234
01:05:58.780 --> 01:06:05.280
They had no idea the paces that they would put people through to make it realized on screen.

1235
01:06:05.960 --> 01:06:07.840
It was just a funny idea.

1236
01:06:08.120 --> 01:06:09.440
Oh, you know what would be funny?

1237
01:06:09.760 --> 01:06:14.140
They wrestle in these sumo suits and then Ed falls in the water and floats away.

1238
01:06:14.560 --> 01:06:17.780
I mean, the amount of effort that went into making that happen.

1239
01:06:18.200 --> 01:06:18.780
I know.

1240
01:06:18.880 --> 01:06:20.620
I think about this all the time with our scripts.

1241
01:06:20.760 --> 01:06:21.620
Like, oh, it'll be funny.

1242
01:06:21.700 --> 01:06:23.080
We'll have a funeral for a bird.

1243
01:06:23.200 --> 01:06:26.020
But we need a specialized tiny coffin made.

1244
01:06:26.620 --> 01:06:26.860
Yeah.

1245
01:06:27.140 --> 01:06:29.300
And several dead birds to choose from.

1246
01:06:29.500 --> 01:06:29.740
Mm-hmm.

1247
01:06:30.340 --> 01:06:34.100
And then Jim and Karen sneak off and they're sneaking off to call David.

1248
01:06:34.240 --> 01:06:35.300
But Pam doesn't know that.

1249
01:06:35.420 --> 01:06:37.780
But you know she saw them walk away.

1250
01:06:37.960 --> 01:06:38.680
You know she did.

1251
01:06:38.880 --> 01:06:39.620
She's aware.

1252
01:06:39.820 --> 01:06:39.900
Yeah.

1253
01:06:39.900 --> 01:06:43.880
And they have this moment where they, you know, call David.

1254
01:06:44.080 --> 01:06:45.380
Jim says he wants to be considered.

1255
01:06:45.620 --> 01:06:46.720
And then Karen does.

1256
01:06:46.920 --> 01:06:51.520
And then Jim is like teasing her because she sort of fumbles her words, right?

1257
01:06:51.660 --> 01:06:52.680
When she calls David.

1258
01:06:53.600 --> 01:06:54.040
Yeah.

1259
01:06:54.120 --> 01:06:56.920
I didn't think that was cool boyfriend behavior, I have to say.

1260
01:06:57.140 --> 01:06:57.540
Oh, right.

1261
01:06:57.540 --> 01:07:00.360
Because she's trying to leave a professional message, right?

1262
01:07:01.620 --> 01:07:03.880
She's speaking to David Wallace.

1263
01:07:04.080 --> 01:07:07.660
And she's saying she'd like to be considered for this job.

1264
01:07:07.820 --> 01:07:11.820
And he's kind of heckling her during this phone call.

1265
01:07:12.040 --> 01:07:15.080
And I, and he's distracting her.

1266
01:07:15.340 --> 01:07:19.200
And I know I think it was meant to be kind of like cute and playful.

1267
01:07:19.540 --> 01:07:21.740
But I don't know.

1268
01:07:22.580 --> 01:07:28.280
If I'm, if I'm making a professional phone call, I don't appreciate being heckled.

1269
01:07:28.460 --> 01:07:29.360
Don't mess with me.

1270
01:07:30.360 --> 01:07:33.700
Don't mess with me when I'm making an important boss lady call.

1271
01:07:33.880 --> 01:07:36.180
Or is he trying to sabotage?

1272
01:07:36.800 --> 01:07:39.340
He might be trying to sandwich this moment.

1273
01:07:39.540 --> 01:07:40.700
He tried to sandwich it.

1274
01:07:41.220 --> 01:07:44.940
Well, we also mentioned earlier that this was an added scene.

1275
01:07:46.080 --> 01:07:49.540
Originally, these phone calls happened in the office before we went to beach day.

1276
01:07:49.740 --> 01:07:54.780
But they made a change and they decided it was better to reveal this information later.

1277
01:07:55.220 --> 01:07:58.560
But this led to probleMs.

1278
01:07:58.560 --> 01:08:00.980
Like we already had such a packed day.

1279
01:08:01.120 --> 01:08:04.260
And here we were trying to add little moments and add little scenes.

1280
01:08:04.480 --> 01:08:07.940
And Jen actually talked about this as one of the challenges.

1281
01:08:07.960 --> 01:08:11.580
Just trying to get everything shot on these days.

1282
01:08:11.740 --> 01:08:14.740
Because, you know, you have so many things you have to shoot in daylight.

1283
01:08:14.740 --> 01:08:16.580
And then the sun goes down.

1284
01:08:16.760 --> 01:08:18.040
You can't make it stay out.

1285
01:08:18.040 --> 01:08:20.280
Also, these were such big, ambitious days.

1286
01:08:20.280 --> 01:08:28.060
And if we would go past the allotted time, we would get into overtime, which was incredibly expensive for the show.

1287
01:08:28.060 --> 01:08:31.400
Because they would have to pay the crew overtime, the actors overtime.

1288
01:08:31.720 --> 01:08:36.520
And so Kent Zabornak, our line producer, was trying to keep us on our day.

1289
01:08:36.680 --> 01:08:39.640
And he was having to check in with Jen and Harold quite a bit about it.

1290
01:08:39.640 --> 01:08:42.520
But, yeah, here's what she had to say about that.

1291
01:08:42.680 --> 01:08:48.700
And I remember Kent being very patient with us and giving us extra time and adding in cushions.

1292
01:08:48.940 --> 01:08:53.220
But it was still really difficult to get everything that we needed to get done.

1293
01:08:53.480 --> 01:08:55.900
I mean, we were going very fast and working incredibly efficiently.

1294
01:08:55.900 --> 01:08:57.160
It was just hard.

1295
01:08:57.160 --> 01:09:05.640
And I remember there was one moment where Kent had already given us a cushion and maybe even a second cushion.

1296
01:09:05.640 --> 01:09:10.000
And then we were about to go over it.

1297
01:09:10.080 --> 01:09:11.400
We were almost done.

1298
01:09:11.520 --> 01:09:12.660
We were so close to being done.

1299
01:09:12.860 --> 01:09:15.900
But Kent called and I got the call.

1300
01:09:16.180 --> 01:09:20.420
And we were about ready to say, you know, Harold was about ready to call action.

1301
01:09:20.420 --> 01:09:29.060
And Kent is, like, frustrated because he wants us to be done because he's given us extra time and he wants us to be done and completely understandable.

1302
01:09:29.340 --> 01:09:37.280
But I knew that if I kept talking to Kent, we were going to be slowed down by 10 seconds at that point.

1303
01:09:37.300 --> 01:09:40.160
And that 10 seconds would allow us to finish the shot.

1304
01:09:40.360 --> 01:09:47.240
So Kent is talking to me and I very gently and gingerly just put the phone down on a chair.

1305
01:09:47.240 --> 01:09:50.420
And we got the last shot and we were done.

1306
01:09:50.740 --> 01:09:57.640
It was definitely this weird moment of, like, this is the producer of your show telling you that you need to stop shooting.

1307
01:09:57.640 --> 01:10:02.420
And I realized I can either talk to him about the fact that we're going to stop shooting or get the shot.

1308
01:10:02.660 --> 01:10:05.360
So we got the shot.

1309
01:10:05.540 --> 01:10:09.080
It was definitely one of those moments of, like, ha, hope that was cool.

1310
01:10:10.360 --> 01:10:16.600
Angela, I think that what Jen is talking about is a little bit of sabotage.

1311
01:10:16.600 --> 01:10:17.900
Is she sabotaging?

1312
01:10:19.180 --> 01:10:20.640
She's not sabotaging.

1313
01:10:20.940 --> 01:10:25.240
She's sabotaging her phone call so that they could get these shots.

1314
01:10:25.800 --> 01:10:27.200
I think it's pretty amazing.

1315
01:10:27.440 --> 01:10:31.440
Like, I can sort of just visualize Kent going on and on, like, Jen, you can't keep adding scenes.

1316
01:10:31.540 --> 01:10:35.620
And she just set the phone down and was like, yes, do it one more time.

1317
01:10:35.840 --> 01:10:37.660
One more time while he's venting.

1318
01:10:38.480 --> 01:10:39.280
Yes, exactly.

1319
01:10:39.280 --> 01:10:48.480
Well, you guys, part of Kent's job, he has such a hard job because he answers to both Greg, who is the creative voice of the show.

1320
01:10:48.680 --> 01:10:53.000
And, you know, his job is to make all of Greg's dreams come true creatively.

1321
01:10:53.000 --> 01:10:58.500
But he also answers to the network, who has given him a budget and a timetable.

1322
01:10:58.600 --> 01:11:00.380
And you can't spend more money than this.

1323
01:11:00.560 --> 01:11:04.580
And everything that you said earlier, Angela, about staying on time.

1324
01:11:04.800 --> 01:11:14.620
Because Kent will look at a script and he will try to estimate, based on conversations with the director and the writer and the crew, how long each scene will take.

1325
01:11:14.620 --> 01:11:19.020
So he'll say, all right, we need three hours to film Ed at Sea.

1326
01:11:19.480 --> 01:11:23.580
But I'm only going to give 45 minutes to Jim and Karen in the sand.

1327
01:11:23.800 --> 01:11:24.340
That's right.

1328
01:11:24.400 --> 01:11:29.880
And if any of these things go over even 10 minutes, they have to steal 10 minutes from another scene.

1329
01:11:30.180 --> 01:11:33.060
And it becomes just this logistical nightmare.

1330
01:11:33.340 --> 01:11:35.300
And Kent has to keep an eye on all of it.

1331
01:11:35.440 --> 01:11:37.980
And it is a vicious domino effect, too.

1332
01:11:38.220 --> 01:11:40.920
Once you get off, it just spirals your whole day.

1333
01:11:40.920 --> 01:11:45.540
I feel like this is like a great metaphor for partnerships.

1334
01:11:45.540 --> 01:11:50.120
Because I feel like in every family, there's one person that's a little bit of the big thinker dreamer.

1335
01:11:50.580 --> 01:11:52.700
On vacation, we'll do all of this.

1336
01:11:52.840 --> 01:11:59.460
And then there's the person in the relationship that's like, I've allotted $80 for things at the gift shop.

1337
01:11:59.660 --> 01:12:01.420
That's all we're spending at the gift shop, OK?

1338
01:12:01.900 --> 01:12:08.200
Because if we spend more than $80 at the gift shop, we can't do that extra bungee cord thing that you all want to do.

1339
01:12:08.700 --> 01:12:09.220
Yes.

1340
01:12:10.140 --> 01:12:12.040
That is exactly Kent.

1341
01:12:12.300 --> 01:12:13.480
He is that person.

1342
01:12:14.300 --> 01:12:15.460
Keep us on budget.

1343
01:12:15.720 --> 01:12:16.600
Keep us on time.

1344
01:12:16.800 --> 01:12:16.940
Yep.

1345
01:12:17.700 --> 01:12:21.700
Well, now, Angela, it is time for the coal walk.

1346
01:12:21.840 --> 01:12:23.180
Talk about a big scene.

1347
01:12:23.400 --> 01:12:25.160
This was a big evening for us.

1348
01:12:25.300 --> 01:12:27.340
It was a very ambitious shoot.

1349
01:12:27.340 --> 01:12:28.460
We had a lot to cover.

1350
01:12:28.600 --> 01:12:31.140
We could not start till it got dark.

1351
01:12:31.680 --> 01:12:32.100
Yep.

1352
01:12:32.500 --> 01:12:34.800
And there was a lot of prep.

1353
01:12:35.040 --> 01:12:37.820
I don't know if you remember, Jenna, how they made those coals.

1354
01:12:38.000 --> 01:12:38.400
Do you remember?

1355
01:12:39.220 --> 01:12:40.940
It was multi-layered.

1356
01:12:41.060 --> 01:12:46.220
And we had a lot of people ask this question about how did you make the coals?

1357
01:12:46.360 --> 01:12:53.660
We got mail from Ellie Troutman, Chandra M., Jamie S., Heather Donnelly, Renee R., and many others.

1358
01:12:53.660 --> 01:12:59.200
They wanted to know, how did the props department create the hot coals?

1359
01:12:59.320 --> 01:13:00.700
They looked so real.

1360
01:13:01.000 --> 01:13:03.080
Because, guys, these were not real coals.

1361
01:13:03.320 --> 01:13:03.560
Yes.

1362
01:13:03.680 --> 01:13:06.200
So we could not have a huge fire here, you guys.

1363
01:13:06.200 --> 01:13:09.520
We were allowed to have the tiki torches and little small flames.

1364
01:13:09.520 --> 01:13:15.240
For example, when the pile of wood burst into flames, that's all special effects.

1365
01:13:15.480 --> 01:13:17.120
That was not at all a fire.

1366
01:13:17.280 --> 01:13:23.400
They put that in in post because there were fire regulations at this particular park, right?

1367
01:13:23.780 --> 01:13:27.520
Well, there are fire regulations in California in general.

1368
01:13:27.520 --> 01:13:29.040
Yes, yes, absolutely.

1369
01:13:29.420 --> 01:13:34.620
So Harold Ramis talked a little bit about this coal fire pit on the DVD commentary.

1370
01:13:34.700 --> 01:13:40.920
He said they started, first of all, by digging a big hole in the ground.

1371
01:13:40.920 --> 01:13:48.040
And then they buried in the ground a light box, just a big box that lit up.

1372
01:13:48.140 --> 01:13:55.760
And then they put on top of the light box these little yellow and orange gels to make it look

1373
01:13:55.760 --> 01:13:56.460
like a flame.

1374
01:13:56.460 --> 01:14:03.520
And then on top of those light gels, on top of the light box, they put little lava rocks.

1375
01:14:03.520 --> 01:14:10.860
And then running on either side of this hole in the ground, they had put two gas lines with

1376
01:14:10.860 --> 01:14:15.380
very small little flickers of real fire that popped out of the ground.

1377
01:14:16.260 --> 01:14:16.900
Yes.

1378
01:14:17.060 --> 01:14:23.540
And because there was real fire as part of the coal walk, we had a very big safety meeting

1379
01:14:23.540 --> 01:14:24.140
about that.

1380
01:14:24.360 --> 01:14:25.160
One of our biggeSt.

1381
01:14:25.720 --> 01:14:28.040
Yeah, for sure.

1382
01:14:28.300 --> 01:14:33.420
It's particularly important because, as you know, rain, as Dwight,

1383
01:14:33.520 --> 01:14:38.420
is going to walk onto these hot coals, stand there, and then fall to the ground.

1384
01:14:38.420 --> 01:14:40.600
And that was real fire next to him.

1385
01:14:40.740 --> 01:14:41.900
On either side of him.

1386
01:14:42.000 --> 01:14:42.260
Yes.

1387
01:14:42.440 --> 01:14:47.460
And I remember when rain did that, one of the most painful things for rain was being barefoot

1388
01:14:47.460 --> 01:14:48.540
on the lava rocks.

1389
01:14:48.760 --> 01:14:49.380
It hurt.

1390
01:14:49.560 --> 01:14:51.360
It's like stepping on a Lego.

1391
01:14:51.540 --> 01:14:52.480
You know how that feels.

1392
01:14:52.780 --> 01:14:55.620
I do know how it feels because I did it as well.

1393
01:14:55.740 --> 01:14:55.940
Yeah.

1394
01:14:56.300 --> 01:15:02.560
In fact, I'll say that people have mentioned that I look like my feet really hurt after I walk

1395
01:15:02.560 --> 01:15:06.200
across the hot coals and they've wondered if I really walked on hot coals.

1396
01:15:06.360 --> 01:15:12.920
No, my feet were sore and bruised from having to run across lava rocks over and over again.

1397
01:15:13.000 --> 01:15:13.160
Yeah.

1398
01:15:13.340 --> 01:15:14.880
Like my feet did kind of hurt.

1399
01:15:15.060 --> 01:15:19.520
Throw a bunch of like hard Legos on the ground and run back and forth over them.

1400
01:15:20.000 --> 01:15:21.960
That's as good as a coal walk right there.

1401
01:15:22.580 --> 01:15:23.760
A Lego walk.

1402
01:15:24.020 --> 01:15:26.760
If you want that experience, you can do it at home.

1403
01:15:26.760 --> 01:15:29.600
Michael wanted everyone to walk over the hot coals.

1404
01:15:29.740 --> 01:15:31.260
We said he has to go firSt.

1405
01:15:31.440 --> 01:15:36.040
And Steve's hesitation as Michael to go over those coals, Jenna, was so brilliant.

1406
01:15:36.380 --> 01:15:43.080
His whole body posture, the expression on his face, and he improvised the line,

1407
01:15:43.280 --> 01:15:46.160
the mind has to wrap around the foot.

1408
01:15:47.340 --> 01:15:51.550
That was an improvisation.

1409
01:15:51.670 --> 01:15:52.690
I did not look that up.

1410
01:15:52.790 --> 01:15:53.790
I love knowing that.

1411
01:15:53.790 --> 01:15:59.490
And we have a call back to this whole runner of Angela being grossed out by Kevin's feet.

1412
01:16:00.430 --> 01:16:01.890
Yes, that's right.

1413
01:16:02.070 --> 01:16:04.950
She's like, I am not going to go after you and your gross feet.

1414
01:16:05.130 --> 01:16:07.730
And he's like, Angela, it's like a thousand degrees.

1415
01:16:09.610 --> 01:16:16.350
One of my favorite moments in this whole sequence is when Michael says to the group,

1416
01:16:16.350 --> 01:16:21.970
he's so disappointed that no one has walked across the coals except sort of Dwight.

1417
01:16:22.610 --> 01:16:25.110
And he says, you know what?

1418
01:16:25.470 --> 01:16:30.830
It's clear to me that if I had to name my replacement today, it would be Mr. Outside Hire.

1419
01:16:31.150 --> 01:16:34.750
And then Angela says, or Mrs. Outside Hire.

1420
01:16:34.910 --> 01:16:38.890
And Michael is like, yes, or that.

1421
01:16:39.750 --> 01:16:40.870
He's so good.

1422
01:16:44.840 --> 01:16:46.980
I know, but a little bit of Angela sass there.

1423
01:16:47.280 --> 01:16:48.440
Like I pointed my finger.

1424
01:16:49.060 --> 01:16:49.440
Mm-hmm.

1425
01:16:49.640 --> 01:16:49.880
Yeah.

1426
01:16:50.460 --> 01:16:53.400
Wait, Jenna, we do have a background catch.

1427
01:16:53.560 --> 01:16:54.460
We can't pass up.

1428
01:16:54.560 --> 01:16:55.220
It's too good.

1429
01:16:55.840 --> 01:16:56.360
Say it.

1430
01:16:56.460 --> 01:16:59.800
While Michael's going on and on about who won't walk across the coals.

1431
01:16:59.900 --> 01:17:01.840
And he's like, and Andy is never here today.

1432
01:17:02.260 --> 01:17:04.900
It pans past us to the water.

1433
01:17:05.960 --> 01:17:07.960
Andy has floated into the marsh.

1434
01:17:08.160 --> 01:17:10.080
He's stuck on his back.

1435
01:17:10.440 --> 01:17:12.700
Poor Ed in the dark, in the lake.

1436
01:17:12.860 --> 01:17:18.340
And at 19 minutes, 55 seconds, you can see his wetsuit on his arm.

1437
01:17:18.480 --> 01:17:18.600
Oh.

1438
01:17:18.600 --> 01:17:19.740
Yeah, on his arm.

1439
01:17:19.940 --> 01:17:20.100
Oh.

1440
01:17:20.100 --> 01:17:22.740
His arm is like extended out of his sumo suit.

1441
01:17:22.880 --> 01:17:27.400
And you can see the beige wetsuit that he had to put on because it was so cold.

1442
01:17:27.400 --> 01:17:44.140
And then people wrote in Angela, and they said, how come in season nine, when Pam is crying, the documentary crew helps her, but when Andy is floating away at night,

1443
01:17:44.440 --> 01:17:47.540
they just turn off the camera lights and walk away.

1444
01:17:47.540 --> 01:17:48.260
I know.

1445
01:17:48.860 --> 01:17:49.660
I know.

1446
01:17:49.960 --> 01:17:51.980
That's one of the questions Jack had.

1447
01:17:52.160 --> 01:17:55.620
My stepson, he was like, why did the camera crew not help him?

1448
01:17:56.120 --> 01:17:56.780
I know.

1449
01:17:56.880 --> 01:17:57.920
And I was like, you know what?

1450
01:17:57.980 --> 01:18:03.320
At that moment, they were like, we can't disturb the natural course of the story.

1451
01:18:03.320 --> 01:18:05.240
We must just document it.

1452
01:18:06.060 --> 01:18:08.460
Well, we're coming up to this big moment.

1453
01:18:08.940 --> 01:18:11.640
There is a huge moment about to happen for Pam.

1454
01:18:12.200 --> 01:18:12.760
Yeah.

1455
01:18:12.900 --> 01:18:16.200
She wants to run across the coals, and Michael says, no, no.

1456
01:18:16.760 --> 01:18:17.240
Yeah.

1457
01:18:17.440 --> 01:18:21.220
And then he moves them on, and he wants them all to speak.

1458
01:18:21.440 --> 01:18:24.980
Dwight is doing his version of the aristocrats very badly.

1459
01:18:25.900 --> 01:18:29.500
And in the background, Pam runs across the coals.

1460
01:18:30.480 --> 01:18:31.040
Yeah.

1461
01:18:31.040 --> 01:18:34.060
And this just feeds her soul.

1462
01:18:34.300 --> 01:18:36.840
She feels so alive in this moment.

1463
01:18:37.140 --> 01:18:43.960
She did this one thing for herself, and she runs over to the group, and she wants to share.

1464
01:18:45.060 --> 01:18:45.700
Yeah.

1465
01:18:45.980 --> 01:18:51.480
And she gives this speech, and everything just comes pouring out.

1466
01:18:51.800 --> 01:18:55.360
Her disappointment that people didn't come to the art show.

1467
01:18:55.360 --> 01:19:09.240
But more than that, her feelings for Jim and how much she misses him and the truth, the big truth, which is that while there were many reasons to call off her wedding to Roy,

1468
01:19:09.620 --> 01:19:14.380
the reason she did it was because of how she feels about Jim.

1469
01:19:15.040 --> 01:19:15.520
Yeah.

1470
01:19:16.500 --> 01:19:16.660
Yeah.

1471
01:19:16.660 --> 01:19:17.300
And oof.

1472
01:19:17.720 --> 01:19:22.340
First of all, I sat next to John in this scene.

1473
01:19:22.800 --> 01:19:24.240
Angela and Jim were side by side.

1474
01:19:24.320 --> 01:19:25.440
I don't know if you noticed that.

1475
01:19:25.700 --> 01:19:26.420
I did.

1476
01:19:26.420 --> 01:19:31.160
And so when you were talking to Jim, I was right in your eyeline.

1477
01:19:31.900 --> 01:19:38.420
You know, I couldn't believe my luck, honestly, because this is such a beautiful performance by you, Jenna.

1478
01:19:38.680 --> 01:19:40.960
You do such a great job.

1479
01:19:40.960 --> 01:19:58.280
It's so moving to watch you as Pam transition through all of those beats, and I had this out-of-body experience as your friend watching you in awe give this fantastic performance,

1480
01:19:58.280 --> 01:20:04.580
wanting to tear up and cry because my friend was crushing it.

1481
01:20:04.580 --> 01:20:16.880
And then having to be Angela Martin, who would never react to you that way, and then at the same time, just being in awe of this moment.

1482
01:20:16.900 --> 01:20:20.020
It was just a beautiful thing to watch, Jenna.

1483
01:20:20.180 --> 01:20:23.060
You did such a great job, and you had to do it over and over.

1484
01:20:23.180 --> 01:20:30.860
We did several takes because they had to get people's reactions, and every time you crushed it, every single time.

1485
01:20:31.980 --> 01:20:39.580
Well, a lot of people ask if that speech was improvised or partially improvised, and it was not.

1486
01:20:39.880 --> 01:20:48.820
That was all written, and as Jen shared with us, written by Greg Daniels, and I memorized it and delivered it word for word.

1487
01:20:49.880 --> 01:21:02.000
I worked on it for a very long time, and I remember trying to think about those times in my life where you've done something that give you a burst of adrenaline.

1488
01:21:05.000 --> 01:21:08.940
And that's what I wanted her to come into the speech with.

1489
01:21:08.940 --> 01:21:19.660
She's just done this cold walk, and she has this burst of adrenaline, and she doesn't even think about that she's going to share.

1490
01:21:19.880 --> 01:21:21.220
It's completely different.

1491
01:21:21.360 --> 01:21:30.040
This is very impulsive, and that was kind of what I put behind the speech, but I knew that speech backwards and forwards.

1492
01:21:30.040 --> 01:21:41.360
I could have delivered it for days because I also know as an actor, at least for me especially, the more I know the material, the less I have to think of the word.

1493
01:21:42.560 --> 01:21:44.840
And it can just be about the performance.

1494
01:21:44.860 --> 01:21:47.040
I never had to search for it.

1495
01:21:47.160 --> 01:21:47.740
I had it.

1496
01:21:47.840 --> 01:21:50.260
It was like on a little tape in my head.

1497
01:21:50.580 --> 01:21:51.820
That's exactly right.

1498
01:21:51.820 --> 01:21:58.780
You don't have to worry about what you're saying, and you can just really experience the moment and feel it because the words are all there.

1499
01:21:59.340 --> 01:22:10.000
And I just thought it was brilliant, and I thought everyone's reactions were great because there is a dance that you do on a show like The Office.

1500
01:22:10.000 --> 01:22:17.780
There is the person who is driving the scene, who's putting out all this information, and then the other side of the story is the people reacting to it.

1501
01:22:18.040 --> 01:22:19.960
And the two parts are very important.

1502
01:22:19.960 --> 01:22:22.520
And I thought that John is Jim.

1503
01:22:22.800 --> 01:22:23.760
His reactions were great.

1504
01:22:23.960 --> 01:22:27.020
Everyone in the crowd, they cut to Rashida.

1505
01:22:27.400 --> 01:22:28.340
Oh, my God.

1506
01:22:28.540 --> 01:22:31.580
Her look as Karen was amazing.

1507
01:22:31.800 --> 01:22:34.500
It was like, I can't believe this is happening.

1508
01:22:34.820 --> 01:22:35.800
Oh, my God.

1509
01:22:36.060 --> 01:22:37.240
It was everything.

1510
01:22:37.800 --> 01:22:39.420
And also heartbreak.

1511
01:22:39.640 --> 01:22:40.700
It was all of it.

1512
01:22:41.720 --> 01:22:41.840
Yeah.

1513
01:22:42.440 --> 01:22:47.220
Well, and you mentioned about what it's like to be the person driving a scene.

1514
01:22:47.220 --> 01:22:50.620
This was kind of my first experience with that.

1515
01:22:50.800 --> 01:22:52.320
And it was this huge speech.

1516
01:22:52.320 --> 01:22:55.120
And that's another reason why I made sure I knew every single word.

1517
01:22:55.420 --> 01:23:00.720
And I got a real glimpse at what Steve does every single day, every single week.

1518
01:23:01.980 --> 01:23:02.420
Wow.

1519
01:23:03.560 --> 01:23:05.600
Delivering these big speeches to the group.

1520
01:23:05.840 --> 01:23:07.620
I mean, all your eyeballs were on me.

1521
01:23:07.700 --> 01:23:08.480
I was so nervous.

1522
01:23:09.280 --> 01:23:09.380
Yeah.

1523
01:23:09.460 --> 01:23:10.400
It's a lot of work.

1524
01:23:10.400 --> 01:23:11.500
It's a lot of work.

1525
01:23:11.620 --> 01:23:15.120
And not just the cast watching you, the whole crew.

1526
01:23:15.260 --> 01:23:16.000
It's everything.

1527
01:23:16.880 --> 01:23:17.160
Yeah.

1528
01:23:17.640 --> 01:23:17.940
Yeah.

1529
01:23:17.940 --> 01:23:19.660
Well, I thought it was so great.

1530
01:23:19.900 --> 01:23:21.280
I just loved it so much.

1531
01:23:21.780 --> 01:23:22.700
Well, thank you.

1532
01:23:22.900 --> 01:23:24.940
You know, I hadn't seen it since it first aired.

1533
01:23:24.940 --> 01:23:28.400
And when I watched it, I was really proud of myself.

1534
01:23:28.560 --> 01:23:29.260
You should be.

1535
01:23:29.480 --> 01:23:31.400
I was like, oh, my gosh.

1536
01:23:31.420 --> 01:23:34.080
I love that that performance is on tape.

1537
01:23:34.080 --> 01:23:37.220
And I can watch it later because it really made me feel like a good actress.

1538
01:23:37.460 --> 01:23:39.420
I was like, oh, look at me go.

1539
01:23:39.420 --> 01:23:40.720
You are a good actress.

1540
01:23:40.920 --> 01:23:41.960
I was so proud of myself.

1541
01:23:41.980 --> 01:23:43.280
You should feel that way.

1542
01:23:43.400 --> 01:23:44.000
You should.

1543
01:23:45.260 --> 01:23:50.180
Well, I was always I was always curious why they sat me next to Jim and didn't have Karen

1544
01:23:50.180 --> 01:23:50.920
next to him.

1545
01:23:52.160 --> 01:23:57.920
And then that moment ends with Jim looking straight ahead and me kind of looking off to the side

1546
01:23:57.920 --> 01:23:59.320
like, oh, my God.

1547
01:24:00.680 --> 01:24:04.860
Yeah, I think you are there to be the audience reflected.

1548
01:24:05.420 --> 01:24:05.540
Right.

1549
01:24:05.720 --> 01:24:08.460
You're what everyone at home is like, whoa.

1550
01:24:08.460 --> 01:24:14.820
So I think it was good to have you there being that other piece of the conversation.

1551
01:24:15.340 --> 01:24:15.440
Yeah.

1552
01:24:15.940 --> 01:24:19.920
But, you know, the episode was supposed to end there in the script.

1553
01:24:20.200 --> 01:24:21.580
That's the end of the episode.

1554
01:24:22.360 --> 01:24:26.660
But we added a little tag back on the bus singing on the bus.

1555
01:24:26.820 --> 01:24:29.400
And this tag confused a lot of people.

1556
01:24:30.020 --> 01:24:31.380
Well, it's daytime.

1557
01:24:31.920 --> 01:24:32.940
We're clearly happy.

1558
01:24:33.660 --> 01:24:34.720
None of this has happened.

1559
01:24:34.960 --> 01:24:37.700
It doesn't make any time sequential sense.

1560
01:24:37.920 --> 01:24:38.060
Right.

1561
01:24:38.760 --> 01:24:39.160
Yes.

1562
01:24:39.440 --> 01:24:41.700
Everyone wanted to know, did you sleep at the beach?

1563
01:24:41.860 --> 01:24:43.120
You're all in the same clothes.

1564
01:24:43.800 --> 01:24:47.760
How does everyone look like they've bathed if they spent the night at the beach?

1565
01:24:48.600 --> 01:24:50.500
They just threw this tag on the end.

1566
01:24:50.640 --> 01:24:53.160
It was an extra little bit of fun from the episode.

1567
01:24:53.160 --> 01:24:55.120
It was not meant to be sequential.

1568
01:24:55.760 --> 01:24:55.900
Yeah.

1569
01:24:55.960 --> 01:24:58.960
We did not spend the night at the beach story wise.

1570
01:24:59.320 --> 01:24:59.540
Yeah.

1571
01:24:59.740 --> 01:25:01.380
They just in editing, they liked it.

1572
01:25:01.480 --> 01:25:03.460
They like to end on this sort of upbeat.

1573
01:25:03.720 --> 01:25:06.540
You know, there's this huge emotional moment has just happened.

1574
01:25:06.540 --> 01:25:08.500
And then it's us all as a group.

1575
01:25:08.680 --> 01:25:14.160
And Ed said in the DVD commentary that he started the song, The Flintstones on the bus.

1576
01:25:15.500 --> 01:25:18.960
And for him, it was a nod to planes, trains, and automobiles.

1577
01:25:18.960 --> 01:25:21.180
Which I love.

1578
01:25:21.720 --> 01:25:22.820
I love that too.

1579
01:25:23.080 --> 01:25:25.860
You know, they do something similar in the Valentine's Day episode.

1580
01:25:26.060 --> 01:25:31.220
They had that little bit of extra footage of Steve improvising in front of the theater.

1581
01:25:31.480 --> 01:25:35.560
And they threw that bit on the end as a tag for Valentine's Day.

1582
01:25:35.560 --> 01:25:37.180
So it's just something we did sometimes.

1583
01:25:37.440 --> 01:25:37.580
Yeah.

1584
01:25:37.720 --> 01:25:42.380
And I think you can get away with that in a cold open and a tag.

1585
01:25:42.580 --> 01:25:44.880
They sort of can be their little standalone moments.

1586
01:25:45.580 --> 01:25:52.120
But Jenna, before we wrap this up, I wrote about some of my memories of filming this episode in my journal.

1587
01:25:52.660 --> 01:25:59.100
And I said one of the things I loved is that Rashida and I had our trailers side by side in the parking lot.

1588
01:25:59.980 --> 01:26:02.000
And we weren't very far away.

1589
01:26:02.000 --> 01:26:04.680
We were just really close to the catering truck.

1590
01:26:05.300 --> 01:26:09.780
And so in the morning, Harold Ramis would get his coffee and gin.

1591
01:26:10.060 --> 01:26:12.280
And they would have to walk past Rashida and I.

1592
01:26:12.400 --> 01:26:17.460
And we would sit on like the front steps of my trailer like it was our front stoop, right?

1593
01:26:17.900 --> 01:26:18.240
Yeah.

1594
01:26:18.420 --> 01:26:21.660
And we would visit in the mornings with Harold and Jen.

1595
01:26:21.800 --> 01:26:26.460
And I have a photo of us and, you know, having our coffee in the morning.

1596
01:26:26.740 --> 01:26:28.000
I just love it.

1597
01:26:28.000 --> 01:26:28.720
I love that.

1598
01:26:28.960 --> 01:26:29.320
I love that.

1599
01:26:29.320 --> 01:26:31.080
I love that memory so much.

1600
01:26:31.300 --> 01:26:36.800
I don't know if we can say enough what an honor and pleasure it was to work with him.

1601
01:26:37.560 --> 01:26:42.420
But Jen also mentioned this in one of her audio clips.

1602
01:26:42.640 --> 01:26:42.780
Yeah.

1603
01:26:42.960 --> 01:26:46.300
What it meant to her to have the opportunity to work with him.

1604
01:26:46.420 --> 01:26:51.160
I'm so glad he was the person in charge of this crazy, crazy episode.

1605
01:26:51.780 --> 01:26:54.080
Yes, because he was such a delight.

1606
01:26:54.320 --> 01:26:56.940
Jenna, I remembered as I was going through photos.

1607
01:26:56.940 --> 01:27:01.800
Do you remember, because him and Jen had to be kind of far away where we were filming,

1608
01:27:02.040 --> 01:27:06.820
that he had a microphone and he would give sort of big general notes on this microphone.

1609
01:27:07.220 --> 01:27:09.880
And then we would yell at him to tell us jokes.

1610
01:27:10.020 --> 01:27:11.440
And he would tell us jokes.

1611
01:27:11.440 --> 01:27:17.060
And in the DVD, Rain, they said that Rain would say, tell us more about Groundhog Day.

1612
01:27:17.380 --> 01:27:24.380
He would share little bits of trivia on this microphone to us, you know, as we were way far away on the beach.

1613
01:27:24.380 --> 01:27:27.760
And he just made it all so fun.

1614
01:27:28.580 --> 01:27:32.500
Well, Jen mentioned that he was really instrumental in mentoring her.

1615
01:27:32.880 --> 01:27:36.220
And actually, Sam, why don't you play that clip?

1616
01:27:36.520 --> 01:27:37.340
One moment.

1617
01:27:37.540 --> 01:27:45.080
Another thing that happened on that episode was there was a there was a it was a challenging episode because we had a lot to get.

1618
01:27:45.080 --> 01:27:48.660
And and the elements were tricky, too.

1619
01:27:48.900 --> 01:27:53.920
I remember it was like incredibly cold and there were heat lamps and it was also scorching hot sometimes.

1620
01:27:53.920 --> 01:27:55.940
And within the course of one day.

1621
01:27:56.440 --> 01:27:59.520
So there were a lot of additional challenges that episode.

1622
01:27:59.700 --> 01:28:06.140
And there was one day where I was like, we were not going to make our day by sunset.

1623
01:28:06.140 --> 01:28:11.700
We had just way more to shoot than was actually possible to shoot in that day with our challenges.

1624
01:28:12.440 --> 01:28:16.240
And he said to me at one point, Jen, let's like divide up.

1625
01:28:16.380 --> 01:28:19.600
You work with the actors and I'll do the tech stuff.

1626
01:28:19.800 --> 01:28:22.020
And it was only for a brief amount of time.

1627
01:28:22.020 --> 01:28:38.100
But it was one of just a thrill of mine that I was able to do that and kind of start talking to the actors in a slightly different way and start thinking about the course of being a director.

1628
01:28:38.440 --> 01:28:45.540
And it all started with that episode, him giving me a little bit of that responsibility because there was just too much to get done.

1629
01:28:45.540 --> 01:28:53.680
And I'm so grateful for Harold and it's one of the biggest thrills of my career that I got to work with him.

1630
01:28:54.740 --> 01:28:59.480
And guys, Jen ended up directing two episodes of The Office.

1631
01:28:59.480 --> 01:29:02.080
She directed Crime Aid and The Promotion.

1632
01:29:02.300 --> 01:29:07.880
It just fits so perfectly that he extended that to her and shared that with her.

1633
01:29:08.540 --> 01:29:10.580
And how lucky were we?

1634
01:29:11.300 --> 01:29:11.640
Yeah.

1635
01:29:11.920 --> 01:29:14.180
Well, guys, that's Beach Games.

1636
01:29:14.180 --> 01:29:16.140
That is Beach Games.

1637
01:29:16.420 --> 01:29:18.040
Thank you so much for listening.

1638
01:29:18.300 --> 01:29:20.760
This was so wonderful to rewatch.

1639
01:29:21.140 --> 01:29:22.600
I loved it.

1640
01:29:22.740 --> 01:29:23.700
I loved it.

1641
01:29:24.000 --> 01:29:26.540
This was one of my favorite weeks to prepare.

1642
01:29:27.060 --> 01:29:27.580
Me too.

1643
01:29:27.800 --> 01:29:27.960
For sure.

1644
01:29:28.200 --> 01:29:28.400
Yeah.

1645
01:29:28.560 --> 01:29:29.280
And to shoot.

1646
01:29:29.600 --> 01:29:31.220
We got to live it and relive it.

1647
01:29:31.320 --> 01:29:31.800
What a gift.

1648
01:29:32.000 --> 01:29:34.320
Well, next week we have a really big week.

1649
01:29:34.460 --> 01:29:35.280
It's The Job.

1650
01:29:35.280 --> 01:29:38.060
And we're wrapping up season three.

1651
01:29:38.900 --> 01:29:42.500
Now we're going to divide The Job into two parts because it's an hour-long episode.

1652
01:29:42.500 --> 01:29:47.120
We're going to have The Job Part 1 and The Job Part 2, but it's all going to be good.

1653
01:29:47.540 --> 01:29:48.280
All right, guys.

1654
01:29:48.400 --> 01:29:49.140
We'll see you next week.

1655
01:29:49.200 --> 01:29:49.800
Have a good one.

1656
01:29:49.860 --> 01:29:50.400
We love you.

1657
01:29:51.400 --> 01:29:56.210
Thank you for listening to Office Ladies.

